(7454545, 1179887, 136989, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Challenge: I can''t think of a better way to celebrate Earth Day than with an image full of the greenery and wonder the occasion is designed to protect.  A very fitting entry for this challenge and a great representation of how man (path and bridge) can coexist with the beauty of the natural world (forest). <br/> <br/>Color and Composition: I love how you used the path to lead the eye out to the bridge and deeper into the forest.  Nice exposure! The greenery framing the leading line is well exposed especially given the mixed lighting (sunlight and shadows).  Only one bright spot (on the fern in the lower left) but even that is a minor tidbit.  The placement of the bridge is slightly off from center which is a definite plus. I might have shifted it a bit more but the balance is also quite nice. I like that the bridge doesn''t standout from the picture like a sore thumb and blends into the forest. All that greenery might be overwhelming for some but I agree with hkpdso that the picture is comfortable and peaceful. <br/> <br/>Overall: You''ve got me imaging the sounds of a nearby stream or birds in the boughs. As a sucker for pictures with paths that lead off into the distance ... I''m ready to lace into my boots and discover the wonder around the bend. Beautiful...</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Challenge: I can''t think of a better way to celebrate Earth Day than with an image full of the greenery and wonder the occasion is designed to protect.  A very fitting entry for this challenge and a great representation of how man (path and bridge) can coexist with the beauty of the natural world (forest).  Color and Composition: I love how you used the path to lead the eye out to the bridge and deeper into the forest.  Nice exposure! The greenery framing the leading line is well exposed especially given the mixed lighting (sunlight and shadows).  Only one bright spot (on the fern in the lower left) but even that is a minor tidbit.  The placement of the bridge is slightly off from center which is a definite plus. I might have shifted it a bit more but the balance is also quite nice. I like that the bridge doesn''t standout from the picture like a sore thumb and blends into the forest. All that greenery might be overwhelming for some but I agree with hkpdso that the picture is comfortable and peaceful.  Overall: You''ve got me imaging the sounds of a nearby stream or birds in the boughs. As a sucker for pictures with paths that lead off into the distance ... I''m ready to lace into my boots and discover the wonder around the bend. Beautiful...', '2016-05-12 15:56:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(7455219, 1179941, 59029, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Great subject, can''t fault the dof or filling of frame, colours are spot on...but I think that hot wash of white in the middle of the back cost you in terms of score.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps!<br/> <br/>Susan</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Great subject, can''t fault the dof or filling of frame, colours are spot on...but I think that hot wash of white in the middle of the back cost you in terms of score. Hope this helps! Susan', '2016-05-17 22:33:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(7455248, 1179879, 136989, '<td valign="top">A Critique Club comment from Cia...<br/> <br/>Initial Reaction: Love the colors, especially the grass.  I think I''ve been hiking in places like this before and it feels like a real day experience. Very pleasing...<br/> <br/>Challenge: Pictures of the natural world are indeed fitting for an Earth Day challenge.  The warm gold of the grasses offset by green trees and hazy skies definitely work together well. There are a few water spots that are easily noticeable...outside of a minimal challenge those should definitely go.  (But they might count as an etc in the sensor dust category of the new rule set... I''ve been buried in homework lately so not fully sure on that point.)<br/> <br/>Comments: As mentioned by Leontp, the rule of thirds may have helped you out here.  There is a lot of white space in the sky, a narrower band (I.e., a third of the pic) as the white would have helped balance the picture. (Not to mention would have removed some of those spots.). I love how the warmth of the grasses balance with the cooler tones of tree and sky but the large amount of sky emphasizes how washed out the mountains are.  Of course, since this is an Earth Day click, you could have been going for a hazy look on purpose.  In your comments you mention a pathway ... I am afraid I don''t get the reference as nothing feels like a path to me in the composition.  A path or trail would have been a nice touch and might have offered some leading line potential.  Overall I really like the grasses and the details of the greenish-yellow heads.  There are a few heads that are a bit out of focus and distracting, especially the one right below the tree, but this is a minor nit.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2016-05-17 23:03:00.</i></td>', 'A Critique Club comment from Cia... Initial Reaction: Love the colors, especially the grass.  I think I''ve been hiking in places like this before and it feels like a real day experience. Very pleasing... Challenge: Pictures of the natural world are indeed fitting for an Earth Day challenge.  The warm gold of the grasses offset by green trees and hazy skies definitely work together well. There are a few water spots that are easily noticeable...outside of a minimal challenge those should definitely go.  (But they might count as an etc in the sensor dust category of the new rule set... I''ve been buried in homework lately so not fully sure on that point.) Comments: As mentioned by Leontp, the rule of thirds may have helped you out here.  There is a lot of white space in the sky, a narrower band (I.e., a third of the pic) as the white would have helped balance the picture. (Not to mention would have removed some of those spots.). I love how the warmth of the grasses balance with the cooler tones of tree and sky but the large amount of sky emphasizes how washed out the mountains are.  Of course, since this is an Earth Day click, you could have been going for a hazy look on purpose.  In your comments you mention a pathway ... I am afraid I don''t get the reference as nothing feels like a path to me in the composition.  A path or trail would have been a nice touch and might have offered some leading line potential.  Overall I really like the grasses and the details of the greenish-yellow heads.  There are a few heads that are a bit out of focus and distracting, especially the one right below the tree, but this is a minor nit.', '2016-05-17 23:58:21.000000', '2016-05-18 00:03:00.000000', 0),
(7455279, 1179879, 82822, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by ciaeagle:</b></div><hr/><i> A Critique Club comment from Cia...<br/> <br/>Initial Reaction: Love the colors, especially the grass.  I think I''ve been hiking in places like this before and it feels like a real day experience. Very pleasing...<br/> <br/>Challenge: Pictures of the natural world are indeed fitting for an Earth Day challenge.  The warm gold of the grasses offset by green trees and hazy skies definitely work together well. There are a few water spots that are easily noticeable...outside of a minimal challenge those should definitely go.  (But they might count as an etc in the sensor dust category of the new rule set... I''ve been buried in homework lately so not fully sure on that point.)<br/> <br/>Comments: As mentioned by Leontp, the rule of thirds may have helped you out here.  There is a lot of white space in the sky, a narrower band (I.e., a third of the pic) as the white would have helped balance the picture. (Not to mention would have removed some of those spots.). I love how the warmth of the grasses balance with the cooler tones of tree and sky but the large amount of sky emphasizes how washed out the mountains are.  Of course, since this is an Earth Day click, you could have been going for a hazy look on purpose.  In your comments you mention a pathway ... I am afraid I don''t get the reference as nothing feels like a path to me in the composition.  A path or trail would have been a nice touch and might have offered some leading line potential.  Overall I really like the grasses and the details of the greenish-yellow heads.  There are a few heads that are a bit out of focus and distracting, especially the one right below the tree, but this is a minor nit. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I''ll try to follow the things as stated by you, thanks for your guidance :) <br/></td>', 'Originally posted by ciaeagle: A Critique Club comment from Cia... Initial Reaction: Love the colors, especially the grass.  I think I''ve been hiking in places like this before and it feels like a real day experience. Very pleasing... Challenge: Pictures of the natural world are indeed fitting for an Earth Day challenge.  The warm gold of the grasses offset by green trees and hazy skies definitely work together well. There are a few water spots that are easily noticeable...outside of a minimal challenge those should definitely go.  (But they might count as an etc in the sensor dust category of the new rule set... I''ve been buried in homework lately so not fully sure on that point.) Comments: As mentioned by Leontp, the rule of thirds may have helped you out here.  There is a lot of white space in the sky, a narrower band (I.e., a third of the pic) as the white would have helped balance the picture. (Not to mention would have removed some of those spots.). I love how the warmth of the grasses balance with the cooler tones of tree and sky but the large amount of sky emphasizes how washed out the mountains are.  Of course, since this is an Earth Day click, you could have been going for a hazy look on purpose.  In your comments you mention a pathway ... I am afraid I don''t get the reference as nothing feels like a path to me in the composition.  A path or trail would have been a nice touch and might have offered some leading line potential.  Overall I really like the grasses and the details of the greenish-yellow heads.  There are a few heads that are a bit out of focus and distracting, especially the one right below the tree, but this is a minor nit.   I''ll try to follow the things as stated by you, thanks for your guidance :) ', '2016-05-18 02:29:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(7459877, 1179911, 34486, '<td valign="top"><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1182744" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/34486/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1182744.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/34486/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1182744.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/34486/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1182744.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>Successful hatch of the 4 chicks.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2016-06-12 01:52:54.</i></td>', '  Successful hatch of the 4 chicks.', '2016-06-12 02:52:18.000000', '2016-06-12 02:52:54.000000', 0),
(7474284, 1180064, 62357, '<td valign="top">In principle this is OK, it meets the challenge, but it''s also not very interesting due to the composition.  Coming back to the comments I made earlier, simpler images tend to score better.  This one is quite "busy".   I also believe it could be a little bit sharper, again, watching that shutter speed.  <br/> <br/>With regard to the composition, I think this would benefit greatly if you had crouched down and perhaps been eye level with the tree stump.</td>', 'In principle this is OK, it meets the challenge, but it''s also not very interesting due to the composition.  Coming back to the comments I made earlier, simpler images tend to score better.  This one is quite "busy".   I also believe it could be a little bit sharper, again, watching that shutter speed.   With regard to the composition, I think this would benefit greatly if you had crouched down and perhaps been eye level with the tree stump.', '2016-09-15 15:54:13.000000', NULL, 0);
